ght infrastructure to achieve that  vision and where have we made progress  and what do we need to do next let me  begin with our vision many of the  potential uses of fiber to the home will  have significant impacts on society  foundational to digital innovation fiber  based societies will be equipped to deal  with the inevitable future growth  challenges as our world becomes  increasingly digital and as our  population construct continues to age  for me fibre to the home coupled with  the deep fibre that will be needed to  deliver on the gigabit society that  Europe is aspiring for is not about  generic Internet connectivity instead  it's about laying the foundation for  Europe's children to be able to compete  on a level playing field with their  global peers it's about removing the  barriers to digital innovation so Europe  can be positioned at the forefront of  the world's digital societies it's about  ensuring that Europe has the necessary  infrastructure to permit its industries  to take a leading position in the  emerging arenas of 5g autonomous  vehicles IOT smart grid and more  importantly all the future digital  industries that we haven't yet imagined  they need that future-proof  infrastructure for me this is about  building the bootstrap that will be  needed to kickstart Europe's economy and  ensure that we do not get relegated to  becoming the digital impoverished of  this third decade of this millennium  fiber-to-the-home infrastructures are  about inclusion they are about the  removal of barriers they are about  ensuring that our future innovators are  not restricted by the capabilities of  infrastructure from the last century  it's about ensuring our innovators are  free free to create the next googles or  Amazon's comfortable in the knowledge  that their national infrastructure can  scale to meet the needs that they'll  have to serve as a global economy  fiber-to-the-home infrastructures will  facilitate the transition of digital  workers to home working environments and  will ensure that the application of  telemedicine and care will have a  profound impact on how our aging  societies function in the future in the  case of home working this will bring  many benefits including the ability for  fiber rich communities to retain their  best and brightest rather than suffer  the continuity of traditional brain  drain to the large cities throughout the  world and this has direct positive  impacts ranging from positive GDP growth  in the region's to increases in property  prices to relief in traffic congestion  in our cities and towns while in  parallel allowing those that are faced  with long commutes to clawback  increasingly scarce personal time while  we all reduce our personal impact on the  environment in the case of home based  health applications d congestion of our  already overstretched health care  facilities along with substantial  financial savings to the state can be  anticipated however the largest benefits  will be for the users of these new  telemedicine and telecare services  permitted to remain safely within their  community for much longer as a  productive member of society quality of  life will be substantially improved for  all in parallel the duration of time  spent in elderly care facilities per  individual can be greatly reduced easing  the burden on already existing creaking  resources while in many cases providing  greater preservation of personal wealth  in so many fiber-rich examples the  benefit accruing to society goes  are beyond the direct economic benefits  invested are identified by the investor  community as one of the main recipients  society in general will be the main  beneficiary of the fiber networks being  built to this end it is critical  critically important that society's  understanding of these benefits is  increased and that society starts to  demand change if I look at the mid to  long term challenges facing Europe over  and above the challenges of our aging  population and the increased pressure  that they place on our health care  systems environmental sustainability and  lifting economic productivity must be  core to all government strategies  fiber-to-the-home networks can help to  deliver or enable a significant part of  the solution to each of these challenges  fiber-to-the-home infrastructure is the  capacity to transform public services  like education and government services  but it relies on the enablement of the  right infrastructure to deliver the  appropriate services the public sector  is the perfect anchor tenant for such  networks but it needs to use its power  its power to help move the market and it  can only do that by committing to the  change that is needed changes that are  needed to raise educational standards  while simultaneously increasing the  availability and reducing the cost of a  world-class education for all of  Europe's citizens changes needed to  offset the costs increases driven by our  aging population while simultaneously  improving the quality of life for our  elder citizens changes needed to  eradicate the barriers to innovation by  our digital natives  so that Europe can take lead a  leadership role in the world's digital  future this will involve significant and  serious change it is complex and will  require negotiations across all facets  of society whether it's with teaching  staff and their unions nursing and  medical staff and their unions it won't  happen overnight  however we have started and we have  started well  we are only at the beginning of possible  developments that can be enabled by  fiber capacity most future applications  we cannot even dream of today history  has been consistent history has shown us  that the bandwidth always precedes the  application innovation think about doing  everything we take for granted today  like Netflix and FaceTime or Facebook  live and do it on a one megabit  connection and see how you get on or  better again do it on a dial-up  connection the reality is that the  experience would be so poor that we  would never use those applications let  alone the billions of users that enjoy  them today the bandwidth had to come  first a fun fact to think about in any  given city the traffic that you will see  will typically consist of approximately  30% of cars that are looking for a  parking space it's a fairly simple  example but one that could have a great  impact on mobility in cities connected  cars will be great in themselves but  imagine the possibilities with traffic  management when the cloud has the  capability to detect congestion building  in a city and to slow down traffic from  entering that region similarly if the  cloud can alert the vehicles in that  area to freed up parking spaces that are  in close proximity to them the merger  between Internet of Things and much  better network capacity opens a range of  exciting possibilities some productive  some noble or worthy and some just plain  old-fashioned fun we should not seek to  anticipate history has shown it is  unexpected developments that have the  greatest impact and we must prepare for  them by having the right infrastructure  in place you know in the mobile space 3G  was starting to look like it was going  to be a failure until the iPhone came  along capability first then innovation  then development  so why fiber a lot of people observe  that Wireless solutions will develop the  last hop and data delivery I fully agree  for the record the world is going  wireless however when I look at a  wireless solution they all need to get  on to a fixed infrastructure as quickly  as they can and the faster the wireless  solution the quicker it needs to get off  the airwaves at the fiber-to-the-home  council europe we view our  infrastructure our telecoms network  infrastructure as an ecosystem and as  with any ecosystem there is  collaboration dependency interdependence  as well as rivalry and a richness of  interaction  however fiber fiber is the element that  underpins the overall telecoms ecosystem  it is therefore critical for the  well-being of our sector of our economy  of our societies that we move to fibre  to the home networks as rapidly as  possible to put it out its simplest  communications based on fiber optic  glass is based on pulses of light that  transmit ones and zeros of the digital  age as opposed to the electrical pulses  over copper or via radio wave in a  wireless context nobody is able to beat  the speed of light and hence there is  universal agreement that all fixed  networks will move to fiber some  question the speed at which this  transition will happen and in turn the  urgency of making such a move but that  transition is coming and it is  inevitable make no mistake the  importance of these relationships is key  Sweden moved first to 4G deployment in  Europe but in a study by their regulator  pts they found that the reason Sweden  moved first had very little to do with  the availability of spectrum and had a  lot to do with the availability of  backhaul networks backhaul networks that  match the capacity of the wireless  network namely the availability of fiber  we are doubling down on this  relationship in a 5g world there is not  much point in having Giga gigabit  capacity and single-digit millisecond  latency if you cannot backhaul us on an  equally performant Network so we need a  lot of new network we need a lot of  complementary Network investments where  have we made progress and what do we  need to do next it's important that we  recognize that the policy makers in new  European Commission have made a huge  shift in their thinking for years we  have complained of what we saw as fell  false technological neutrality where  nothing was said regarding technology  choice even though in every access  market in Europe we suffered market  failure in the form of dominance now the  Commission has moved to a true form of  technology neutrality it sets very  ambitious targets unless the  technologies choose themselves of course  we have the headline of gigabit  connectivity to all socio-economic  drivers and 100 megabits available  universally what is the specific  reference to fibre to the home and fibre  to the building or equally performant  networks that makes the real difference  in effect the Commission sets a  performance target by reference to fiber  and accept any technology that can meet  those targets these targets are in the  gigabit society communication but also  in legislative proposals that are  proposed as a formal target for national  regulators to aim for even the very act  of setting very high capacity at fibre  to the home or fibre to the basement or  its equivalent is important and has  direct and immediate consequences for  instance in my home country of Ireland  the government's National Broadband Plan  changed its network specification to  require fibre to the home we see the UK  is now allocated funding that  specifically for fiber to the home  others are following suit and we can  expect to see many more such initiatives  in the coming months and years  public funding is great and it can help  on the margin but it is the free market  in the industry that will have to do the  heavy lifting the legislative proposals  from the Commission are very positive in  our view and match the preferred model  for fixed access regulation very closely  the fiber-to-the-home Council Europe has  long advocated the tree step approach  firstly to set a fiber target and  specify geographically where competition  can work to spur investment and more  importantly where it cannot work second  is to enable a fiber rollout by lowering  the barriers to entry and this can be  true a sensible scheme to share passive  infrastructures and probably most  importantly to ensure that a solution  exists that deals with the in building  wiring third is to create the right  incentives for operators to build where  they can and this often requires that  virtual access remedies are removed or  at least greatly constrained we have  seen this model work in a large number  of markets each with its own variations  and peculiarities but each following  these basic elements and I'm pleased to  see these elements at the heart of the  Commission's new access regulation  proposals this bills and the work policy  makers have done in the last years  helping the industry to find ways to  reduce the cost of deployment through  the cost of cost reduction directive and  others we at the fiber-to-the-home  council Europe are very positive and  very supportive of those efforts and  particularly of the recent proposals and  hope to see their rapid adoption into  law does this mean that everything is  rosy of course not but I am very pleased  to say that we are moving in the right  direction and that there is a stronger  than ever momentum across Europe towards  fiber-to-the-home  good connectivity is not just something  for the rich it's not just for the low  key it's not just for the elite it's  something that every something for  everyone and we need to pay attention to  the digital divide society should not be  judged by the standards of those who  have everything it  should be judged on how it treats those  who are on the margins either  economically or based on their geography  we risk exclusion in the digital world  which may be even more dramatic than in  the analog world there were those who  are at risk of being excluded from a  democratic process that is increasingly  digitized those who are at risk have  been excluded from learning and  healthcare and all the other  improvements that a fiber-to-the-home  broadband network can deliver some seem  to believe the fiber-to-the-home in our  cities would be sufficient but this is  patently not the case would we deny  other essential services to rural areas  perhaps we'd force a lower electrical  voltage onto rural communities or better  again maybe we could get to your rural  communities to generate their own  electricity that make everyone's lives  easier wouldn't us no we wouldn't do  this nor should this be the case for  very high capacity connectivity we need  fibers of the home and we need it  everywhere fibre to the home council  Europe's deployment and operation  committee ran the numbers in 2012 on a  full fibre to the home network  deployment throughout Europe and rerun  the numbers earlier this year the cost  will be well below 150 billion euro over  the next 10 years and if we allow for  displace two other investments the  incremental capex will be very much less  than that number  ubiquitous fibre to the home is feasible  and we must not limit our ambitions the  new broadband platform in the committee  of the region's will leverage local and  regional initiatives while the new  connecting Europe broadband fund will  help to close the funding gap yes we are  calling for the right networks yes we  are calling for the right services as  well and yes we are calling for the  right speeds we're also calling for  inclusion because it's important for  everyone every European citizen to share  the means to improve their lives and  their circumstances no matter where they  are let me conclude with this  fiber-to-the-home can deliver a vision  of the  future which we will see exciting new  services deployed in a range of domains  driving Europe's economy forward in a  sustainable and progressive way we can  only truly rely on these services if  they are built upon the most reliable  networks namely fiber and we must  remember many network elements are  dependent on others so for example we  will not get 5g unless we have fiber  available to support that technology and  its promise of the future  the Commission has recognized this fact  and is legislating to accelerate fiber  investments as the body representing the  industry whose vision is to accelerate  fiber deployment for the good of society  we look forward to working closer with  policymakers to deliver this vision of  the future I'd like to close by once  again welcoming each and every one of  you to the 2017 fiber-to-the-home  conference and welcome you to your  future thank you  [Applause]
 

